{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Who struck you.</b> It may be explained according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory (Rosh HaShanah 3a), who said that the cloud covered Israel and their location was not known to the world, but when Aaron died the clouds of glory departed and the Canaanite came and waged war against Israel. If so, a difficulty arises: from where did Amalek know the place of Israel's encampment, to come to them to wage war against them? Therefore it says <b>who struck you</b> — meaning, chance befell him (קרהו), and he discovered their camp by the way. And its saying <b>and he struck those in the rear of you</b>, etc., means not that the cloud let him enter within Israel's camp, but rather those whom the cloud had cast out — and this is what it says: ״וַיְזַנֵּב בְּךָ״ — those who were stragglers behind you." "<b>And you were faint and weary,</b> etc. — faint still from the servitude of Egypt, and weary from the exertion of the journey." "And its saying <b>and did not fear Elohim</b> — meaning, through this came the faintness into your heart more than the effects of exhaustion and weariness, along the lines of what it says (Isaiah 33:14) ״פָּחֲדוּ בְצִיּוֹן חַטָּאִים״; but had they been righteous they would not have trembled nor feared in battle. And Scripture alerted to the factors that cause him to be hated in our heart, for a great trouble befell Israel, in that he came upon them as the eagle swoops without warning, as is also alluded to in the expression ״קָרְךָ״, and they were weary and faint, and the thing that sustains strength through hidden power was not with them — which was not so in all the other wars, for besides that they did not grow faint or weary in the wars, they were furthermore all of them righteous, God-fearing, lowly of spirit." "<b>Thus concludes the parashah of Ki Tetze.</b>"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :register {:flags {2 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The lemma אשר קרך is rendered 'who struck you' following the verse's plain sense, but the interpretation pivots on the root קרה (to befall/chance), which the commentary makes explicit with יקר מקרהו.", 1 "The lemma is from Deuteronomy 25:18; the 'etc.' (וג׳) covers the rest of the phrase 'and did not fear Elohim,' which the following comment addresses."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:19:07.468176395Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}